1️⃣ DISTINCT — remove duplicates
Returns each value only once, even if it appears many times.

SELECT DISTINCT director FROM movies
ORDER BY director ASC;
Result (duplicates like "John Lasseter" appear once):

director
Andrew Stanton
Brad Bird
Brenda Chapman
Dan Scanlon
John Lasseter
Lee Unkrich
Pete Docter
How it works:

SELECT director alone would repeat John Lasseter 4 times.
DISTINCT applies to all columns after it — SELECT DISTINCT director, year keeps a row only if the combination is unique.
 Without DISTINCT              With DISTINCT
 ┌────────────────┐            ┌────────────────┐
 │ John Lasseter  │            │ John Lasseter  │
 │ John Lasseter  │   ─────►   │ Brad Bird      │
 │ John Lasseter  │            │ Pete Docter    │
 │ Brad Bird      │            │ ...each once   │
 └────────────────┘            └────────────────┘
2️⃣ ORDER BY — sort the result
Sorts rows by one or more columns.

ASC — ascending: A→Z, smallest→largest, oldest→newest (default, can be omitted)
DESC — descending: Z→A, largest→smallest, newest→oldest
Example — newest movies first
SELECT title, year FROM movies
ORDER BY year DESC
LIMIT 4;
Result:

title	year
Monsters University	2013
Brave	2012
Cars 2	2011
Toy Story 3	2010
Example — sort by multiple columns
SELECT title, director, year FROM movies
ORDER BY director ASC, year DESC;
→ Groups by director (A→Z), and inside each director, newest movie first.

Sorting rules:

Text sorts alphabetically, numbers numerically, dates chronologically.
NULL handling differs by database: in PostgreSQL/SQLite, NULLs come last with ASC and first with DESC; in SQL Server they come first with ASC.
3️⃣ LIMIT — return only N rows
Takes the first N rows of the (already sorted) result.

SELECT title FROM movies
ORDER BY title ASC
LIMIT 5;
Result (first 5 alphabetically):

title
A Bug's Life
Brave
Cars 2
Monsters University
The Incredibles
⚠️ LIMIT without ORDER BY is unreliable — the database returns rows in an unspecified order, so "first 5" may differ each run. Almost always use them together.

4️⃣ OFFSET — skip M rows before returning
Used with LIMIT for pagination (page 1, page 2, ...).

SELECT title FROM movies
ORDER BY title ASC
LIMIT 5 OFFSET 5;
→ Skip the first 5 alphabetically, return the next 5.

Result:

title
Toy Story
Toy Story 2
Toy Story 3
Up
WALL-E
Pagination pattern
Page 1:  LIMIT 10 OFFSET 0    → rows 1–10
Page 2:  LIMIT 10 OFFSET 10   → rows 11–20
Page 3:  LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20   → rows 21–30

General formula:  OFFSET = (page_number − 1) × page_size
