5️⃣ TOP — the SQL Server (and MS Access) version
LIMIT doesn't exist in SQL Server. Use TOP right after SELECT instead:

-- SQL Server / MS Access
SELECT TOP 4 title, year FROM movies
ORDER BY year DESC;
Equivalent in MySQL/PostgreSQL/SQLite:

SELECT title, year FROM movies
ORDER BY year DESC
LIMIT 4;
Oracle (older) uses FETCH FIRST / ROWNUM instead:

-- Oracle 12c+
SELECT title, year FROM movies
ORDER BY year DESC
FETCH FIRST 4 ROWS ONLY;
Database	Syntax for "first N rows"
MySQL / PostgreSQL / SQLite	LIMIT N at the end
SQL Server / Access	SELECT TOP N ...
Oracle 12c+	FETCH FIRST N ROWS ONLY
🧭 Logical order of execution (why it matters)
SQL is written in one order but executed in another:

 WRITTEN:              EXECUTED:
 ─────────             ────────────────
 SELECT   →            5. SELECT  (pick columns / apply DISTINCT)
 FROM     →            1. FROM    (get the table)
 WHERE    →            2. WHERE   (filter rows)
 ORDER BY →            3. ORDER BY(sort what's left)
 LIMIT    →            4. LIMIT/OFFSET (trim to N rows)
Key takeaways:

ORDER BY runs before LIMIT — so LIMIT takes the top rows of the sorted result.
DISTINCT applies at SELECT time — with ORDER BY, only columns in the SELECT list can be used reliably.
WHERE filters rows before sorting; HAVING filters groups after aggregation (covered later).
